--- !u!114 &1457715370
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1457715368}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.TextMeshPro::TMPro.TextMeshProUGUI
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: '<b>Two-stage inactivity timeout.</b>

    

    <b>1.</b> Press START.
    The idle 15s countdown begins.

    

    <b>2.</b> Tap, click or drag anywhere
    and it restarts. The ScreenInputZoneManager observes input without consuming
    it, so this keeps working even while you press real buttons.

    

    <b>3.</b>
    If the idle countdown finishes, the overlay appears and a 10s radial countdown
    starts.

    

    <b>4.</b> Touch anywhere during the overlay to dismiss it
    - you go back to step 2.

    

    <b>5.</b> Or let the radial run out. onWarningTimedOut
    fires and the manager stops. What happens next is the consumer''s decision, so
    here it just says so.

    

    <b>6.</b> During the idle countdown, press
    <b>P</b> to pause. It freezes where it is, and input is ignored - a stray tap
    cannot restart a session you deliberately suspended. Press <b>R</b> to carry
    on from the same number.

    

    <i>Why keys and not buttons:</i> pressing
    an on-screen button IS user activity, so the countdown would restart a frame
    before the pause landed. That is correct behaviour, not a bug - in a real app
    you call PauseTimeout from code like when a video starts, with no user input
    involved.

'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
